IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Row il-61. NOUNS of a number cited in a list, of a grant and the place a text puts it, and of a printed date pair. No rule is written here; the facts below are what a reader of the texts could state.
-- TARGET COMPILER: main. No REFUSE, no section GIVEN, no TYPICALLY, no YMD, no sets: a case the sources do not answer is said so in a field, never guessed.
--
-- This row is NOT an encoding of the Compensation to Victims of Hostile Actions Law, 5730-1970 (called "the Law" below; the English titles are the row's renderings, not enacted titles).
-- It records THREE items about what the consolidation of the Law prints, as typed values with tests, each beside what an official instrument prints:
-- item I1, a word in the list of s 4(א) that cites the Disabled Persons Law; item I2, a number in the list of s 7(א) that cites the Soldiers' Families Law; item I3, a grant of 200,000 new shekels.
-- The consolidation is an unofficial aid. The row says "the consolidation prints", never "the Law says", for a line of it.
--
-- THE KINDS BELOW ARE MINE, not the Law's: each is a short plain description I wrote from reading the lines and the pages.
-- The words of the texts are quoted beside each value in the references module (`src:` lines are the consolidation, `ext:[SH ...]` lines are typed from a page image of an official instrument).

§ `Nouns of where a reading comes from`

-- Three kinds of reading, from the strongest to the weakest this row has.
-- The second is the honest name for what the instruments here give: the official page was read, but what the consolidation prints today rests on the consolidation alone.
DECLARE `Where a reading comes from` IS ONE OF
    `an official instrument, read from its page image`
    `an official instrument read from its page image; what the consolidation prints today is CONSOLIDATION-ONLY`
    `the consolidation only`

§ `Nouns of a number cited in a list`

DECLARE `What the consolidation of the law named prints for a cited section` IS ONE OF
    `it prints the section as cancelled`
    `it prints the section with text`
    `not read by this row`

DECLARE `A cited number in a list` HAS
    `the entry` IS A STRING
    `the list it is in` IS A STRING
    `the law the list cites` IS A STRING
    `the consolidation prints it in the list` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an official instruction read by this row deletes it from the list` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an official instruction read by this row adds it to the list` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an official instrument read by this row repeals the section it names` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an official instrument read by this row amends the section it names` IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the consolidation of the law named prints for the section` IS A `What the consolidation of the law named prints for a cited section`
    `the instrument that says so` IS A STRING
    `limb (a) was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`
    `limb (b) was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`

§ `Nouns of a grant and the place a text puts it`

DECLARE `A grant and where a text puts it` HAS
    `the amount in new shekels` IS A NUMBER
    `the law it is put in` IS A STRING
    `the section it is put in` IS A STRING
    `the subsection it is put in` IS A STRING
    `put there by` IS A STRING
    `read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`

§ `Nouns of a printed date pair`

-- A Hebrew date and a Gregorian date printed together. The Hebrew date is written in Latin letters here, so that this file holds no Hebrew that its source does not hold; the Hebrew is on the `ext:` lines.
-- The third field is what the Hebrew-calendar routine in tools/hebcal.py gives for the Hebrew date; the routine is not part of this module.
DECLARE `A printed date pair` HAS
    `the Hebrew date, transliterated` IS A STRING
    `the Gregorian date printed with it` IS A DATE
    `the Gregorian date the calendar routine gives` IS A DATE
    `the pair was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`
